Now I found the actual root cause. Look at `LocationManagerClient.java`, specifically the `determineProvider` method:

```java
private static @Nullable String determineProvider(
    @NonNull LocationManager locationManager,
    @NonNull LocationAccuracy accuracy) {

    final List<String> enabledProviders = locationManager.getProviders(true);

    if (accuracy == LocationAccuracy.lowest) {
        return LocationManager.PASSIVE_PROVIDER;
    } else if (enabledProviders.contains(LocationManager.FUSED_PROVIDER) && Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.S) {
        return LocationManager.FUSED_PROVIDER;
    } else if (enabledProviders.contains(LocationManager.GPS_PROVIDER)) {
        return LocationManager.GPS_PROVIDER;
    } else if (enabledProviders.contains(LocationManager.NETWORK_PROVIDER)) {
        return LocationManager.NETWORK_PROVIDER;
    } else if (!enabledProviders.isEmpty()){
        return enabledProviders.get(0);
    } else {
        return null;
    }
}
```

The method falls through a series of provider checks, prioritizing fused location on Android 12+, then GPS, then network, and finally any available provider. This is where the bug manifests on Android 12 and later.

On Android 12 and above, when the fused provider is available, `LocationManagerClient` defaults to using `LocationManager.FUSED_PROVIDER` instead of GPS. This is Android's built-in OS-level fused location provider, which is distinct from Google Play Services' `FusedLocationProviderClient`. The critical issue is that this OS-level fused provider also enforces a 5-second minimum update interval on most devices, so even with `forceLocationManager: true`, geolocator still gets throttled through the fused provider on newer Android versions.

The real solution is to force `LocationManager.GPS_PROVIDER` directly, which geolocator doesn't expose as an option. I need to either use a Flutter plugin like the `location` package that has a `useGPSProvider` setting, or implement a platform channel to call Android's `LocationManager` with GPS_PROVIDER directly.

Looking at the geolocator code more carefully, the `FUSED_PROVIDER` check only activates on Android 12+ when Google Play Services has integrated with the system, so on a Samsung S23 Ultra running Android 14, `FUSED_PROVIDER` will definitely be available.

This means setting `forceLocationManager: true` doesn't actually change anything on Android 12+ — the `LocationManagerClient` still returns `FUSED_PROVIDER` instead of `GPS_PROVIDER`, and since both are backed by the same underlying fused location provider, they both hit the 5-second floor. The geolocator plugin has no way to bypass this limitation without modifying the plugin itself.

The most practical solution is to patch `geolocator_android` locally using `dependency_overrides` with a modified version that forces `GPS_PROVIDER` selection or adds a dedicated option for it — the actual code change is minimal. straight to `GPS_PROVIDER` when not using lowest accuracy. The simplest approach is removing the `FUSED_PROVIDER` block from `LocationManagerClient.java` so it falls through to `GPS_PROVIDER` instead. Alternatively, I could create a platform channel with Kotlin to call `LocationManager.requestLocationUpdates` directly and stream the results back, or consider using `flutter_background_geolocation` as a replacement, though the user already rejected that option.

A platform channel is probably cleaner for this navigation use case since it isolates the GPS logic to just the nav stream without risking other parts of the app, and it's only about 50 lines total across Kotlin and Dart. I could also use `NMEA` data directly through geolocator's existing `NmeaClient`, but that adds complexity. Let me think through the local patch approach more carefully—I could copy geolocator_android to a local directory and modify `determineProvider` to skip `FUSED_PROVIDER` entirely. dependency on geolocator_android's behavior. But the user wants something working quickly, so maybe the local patch approach is actually better—just copying the library into the project, removing that problematic FUSED_PROVIDER branch, and adding a path override in pubspec.yaml. That's minimal work and keeps everything self-contained without needing to write platform channel code. I'll go with the local patch since it's the fastest path to a working solution. Reordering the provider checks...

